624
00:36:18.036 --> 00:36:21.896
And we believe that recreation is not a discretionary nice to have,

625
00:36:22.496 --> 00:36:26.326
it's a fundamental component of New Zealand's physical and mental health, social

626
00:36:26.416 --> 00:36:28.956
cohesion, youth development, and national identity.

627
00:36:30.096 --> 00:36:34.056
Central government, through the Department of Conservation, is the largest provider

628
00:36:34.116 --> 00:36:36.596
of outdoor recreation opportunities in New Zealand.

629
00:36:37.336 --> 00:36:41.136
Every year, 80% of us visit public conservation lands

630
00:36:41.256 --> 00:36:45.176
to walk, tramp, climb, bike, swim, paddle, and enjoy

631
00:36:45.236 --> 00:36:48.676
a range of other recreation activities that help us connect with nature

632
00:36:49.628 --> 00:36:52.948
The public conservation estate is New Zealand's largest recreation

633
00:36:53.028 --> 00:36:56.768
infrastructure network, and as such, DOC plays a critical role in

634
00:36:56.808 --> 00:37:00.468
ensuring New Zealanders can access quality outdoor recreation

635
00:37:00.588 --> 00:37:03.068
opportunities and connect with our natural environment.

636
00:37:04.908 --> 00:37:08.678
Recreation Aotearoa supports the bill's goal of modernising conservation

637
00:37:08.768 --> 00:37:12.708
management. We recognize that parts of the current system are complex,

638
00:37:12.868 --> 00:37:16.528
slow, and resource intensive. We support efforts to streamline

639
00:37:16.608 --> 00:37:20.208
planning processes, improve concessions administration, and provide

640
00:37:20.388 --> 00:37:23.128
greater consistency across the conservation estate.

641
00:37:24.548 --> 00:37:28.348
We also support proposals to create a national conservation policy statement and

642
00:37:28.408 --> 00:37:32.268
introduce area plans, noting that these,

643
00:37:32.328 --> 00:37:36.188
we believe, need significant amendments as per our submission on

644
00:37:36.248 --> 00:37:37.248
the NCPS.

645
00:37:38.328 --> 00:37:41.788
What we don't support are reforms that would alter the

646
00:37:41.968 --> 00:37:45.548
fundamental purpose of the Conservation Act or the Department of Conservation's

647
00:37:45.628 --> 00:37:45.908
role.

648
00:37:46.828 --> 00:37:50.248
The conservation system must be able to respond to changing visitor

649
00:37:50.258 --> 00:37:54.038
expectations, increasing participation in outdoor recreation,

650
00:37:54.467 --> 00:37:57.728
growing tourism, and the impacts of climate change.

651
00:37:57.988 --> 00:38:01.928
It must also uphold the Crown's obligations under Te Tiriti o Waitangi

652
00:38:02.308 --> 00:38:05.567
and actively support mana whenua to exercise kaitiakitanga.

653
00:38:06.288 --> 00:38:10.038
But modernization of the legislation should improve how the system works,

654
00:38:10.348 --> 00:38:14.328
not change why it exists. As currently drafted, the

655
00:38:14.348 --> 00:38:18.298
bill shifts the longstanding statutory balance within New Zealand's conservation

656
00:38:18.408 --> 00:38:22.078
framework. It fundamentally changes the purpose of conservation

657
00:38:22.168 --> 00:38:22.508
land.

658
00:38:24.108 --> 00:38:28.088
We strongly oppose the so-called economic clauses, specifically

659
00:38:28.148 --> 00:38:32.128
the inclusion of the words "enabling economic activity to the greatest extent

660
00:38:32.288 --> 00:38:36.128
practicable" as a key function of the Department of Conservation

661
00:38:36.268 --> 00:38:40.198
and as a key purpose of the proposed NCPS area plans and

662
00:38:40.248 --> 00:38:41.388
concessions framework.

663
00:38:42.448 --> 00:38:46.008
We acknowledge that economic activity occurs on public conservation land.

664
00:38:46.148 --> 00:38:49.748
We're not opposed to this. We support well-managed tourism, and we

665
00:38:49.788 --> 00:38:53.688
recognize the important role concessionaires play in helping New Zealanders and

666
00:38:53.748 --> 00:38:56.528
visitors access and enjoy public conservation lands.

667
00:38:57.308 --> 00:39:01.148
But conservation of our shared natural and historic heritage must

668
00:39:01.228 --> 00:39:05.188
remain the primary and overriding purpose of public conservation land,

669
00:39:05.788 --> 00:39:09.628
and recreation must continue to be recognized and fostered as a core

670
00:39:09.668 --> 00:39:13.588
public benefit, with economic activity enabled only where it

671
00:39:13.628 --> 00:39:16.768
is consistent with conservation and recreation outcomes.

672
00:39:18.128 --> 00:39:21.848
As such, we urge the committee to recommend that the existing hierarchy within the

673
00:39:21.928 --> 00:39:25.678
act is preserved and that all references to economic activity are

674
00:39:25.748 --> 00:39:29.128
qualified with a clear statement requiring consistency with

675
00:39:29.168 --> 00:39:30.318
conservation outcomes.

676
00:39:31.988 --> 00:39:35.368
Our next point is where we believe the committee has the greatest

677
00:39:35.408 --> 00:39:39.188
opportunity to make a practical and meaningful improvement to the

678
00:39:39.248 --> 00:39:42.898
bill, and that's around the current treatment of not-for-profit outdoor

679
00:39:42.948 --> 00:39:45.728
education providers under the concessions regime.

680
00:39:46.808 --> 00:39:50.688
The way that these providers are currently treated is inequitable and

681
00:39:50.788 --> 00:39:54.777
inconsistent. At present, and rightly so, schools can

682
00:39:54.808 --> 00:39:58.678
take students onto public conservation land without requiring a concession or

683
00:39:58.708 --> 00:40:02.598
incurring the costs and administrative challenges that come with this.

684
00:40:03.408 --> 00:40:06.708
However, not-for-profit outdoor education providers delivering the same

685
00:40:06.788 --> 00:40:09.868
activities to the same students and tertiary

686
00:40:09.888 --> 00:40:13.768
institutions who are training our future recreation, conservation,

687
00:40:13.868 --> 00:40:17.408
tourism, and outdoor education workforce, they are required to hold a

688
00:40:17.448 --> 00:40:20.808
concession. The activities are the same, the students are the

689
00:40:20.868 --> 00:40:24.468
same, the public good, the public benefit is the same, only the

690
00:40:24.488 --> 00:40:28.208
provider differs. In our view, that makes little sense.

691
00:40:29.468 --> 00:40:33.028
Outdoor education is an integral part of New Zealand's education system.

692
00:40:33.488 --> 00:40:37.208
Going on a school camp, doing your first overnight tramp, crossing a

693
00:40:37.248 --> 00:40:41.198
river, visiting a national park for the first time, these are rites

694
00:40:41.308 --> 00:40:43.248
of passage for young people in New Zealand.

695
00:40:44.268 --> 00:40:48.168
They're the experiences that help us build resilience, they

696
00:40:48.208 --> 00:40:51.717
teach leadership and teamwork, and they build environmental awareness, and

697
00:40:51.768 --> 00:40:55.378
oftentimes, they help us develop our sense of identity.

698
00:40:55.448 --> 00:40:58.848
Outdoor education connects young people with the natural environment, and it

699
00:40:58.868 --> 00:41:02.068
fosters a lifelong love of conservation and recreation.

700
00:41:02.748 --> 00:41:06.188
The public conservation estate is also New Zealand's greatest outdoor

701
